  data:  # b696
    kind: work_item  # b696
    title: "Chained method calls emit no call edge, making every Class.method catalog entry unreachable"  # b696
    status: todo_hard  # b696
    priority: 2  # b696
    parent:  # b696
    tags:  # b696
    - adr_0017  # b696
    - taint  # b696
    - analysis_quality  # b696
    isbefore: []  # b696
    duplicate_of: []  # b696
    not_duplicate_of: []  # b696
    pr_ref:  # b696
    description: "FOUND WHILE MEASURING INV-karud CLAUSE (b), 2026-08-03.\n\nThe Python analyzer emits NO call edge for a chained method call. Measured on\na two-line fixture:\n\n    plain = Fernet(key).decrypt(token)\n    Path(\"/tmp/out\").write_bytes(plain)\n\n  emitted edges: instantiates -> Fernet, instantiates -> Path, plus imports.\n  calls edges:   NONE.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE CATALOG, which is the reason this is filed rather than\nnoted: catalog entries written as Class.method are unreachable through this\nshape. That is not a corner case --\n\n  * ALL FOUR built-in python `plaintext` sources are Class.method\n    (Fernet.decrypt, AESGCM.decrypt, AESCCM.decrypt, ChaCha20Poly1305.decrypt)\n  * ALL FOUR built-in python sanitizers are Class.method (.encrypt)\n\nso the shipped crypto catalog cannot exercise a plaintext source-to-sink flow\non Python at all. Clause (b) could only be measured by declaring\nproject-local module-level FUNCTIONS.\n\nThis is the same shape INV-karud's own 2026-08-02 note recorded when its first\npositive control failed (\"a chained call Path(...).write_text(...) which the\nanalyzer does not emit at all\"). It was treated there as a probe-construction\ndetail; it is a catalog-reachability defect.\n\nSCOPE UNMEASURED: whether other languages' analyzers have the same gap, and\nhow much of each language's catalog is Class.method shaped. Count those before\nchoosing a fix."  # b696
    fields: {}  # b696
- op: discuss  # 1625
  at: '2026-08-05T04:13:58Z'  # 1625
  by: agent  # 1625
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1625
  clock: 2  # 1625
  nonce: '1625'  # 1625
  message: "SCOPE MEASURED, as the filing asked (\"count those before choosing a fix\"). Both halves. CATALOGUE HALF, re-measured on the post-#209 catalogue: 469 of 1406 sinks are method-kind (33%), up from the plan-era 337 of 1062 (32%) - the share is stable, the absolute grew. Per language: kotlin 96%, scala 97%, java 97%, objc 90%, swift 88%, rust 53%, python 47%, javascript 35%, go 27%; c/cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell 0%. ANALYZER HALF, measured with a fixture carrying BOTH the chained and the two-step shape per language so the chained case has an in-fixture control, analysed by the real pipeline and run through production _match_propagation_entry against real catalogued sink shapes: java, scala and swift emit and match in BOTH forms; PYTHON emits for two-step and NOTHING for chained, which confirms this item exactly; kotlin and javascript emit for NEITHER form. So THIS ITEM IS PYTHON-SPECIFIC - the chained shape is the defect only here, and the framing does not generalise as its SCOPE UNMEASURED note assumed it might. The kotlin/javascript result is a DIFFERENT defect (external instance-method calls emit no edge at all, swallowing the two-step form too) and is filed separately rather than folded in, because folding a broader per-analyzer gap into a chaining item is the scope-axis error L1 warns about. Positive control held: a module-qualified javascript call (fs.createWriteStream) emits AND matches in the same run that reports nothing for new net.Socket().write(d), so the nulls are evidence rather than a broken harness."  # 1625
